\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Two QRAM idioms, drawn by the quantikz export. Left: a bare \texttt  {Load}---one instruction on the address and data registers, $|a\rangle |d\rangle \DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow |a\rangle |d \oplus M[a]\rangle $, with the table itself a runtime resource. Right: the diagonal block-encoding of an angle table ($D_{jj}=\alpha \cos (s\,d_j/2)$, here $s=\pi /4$, $\alpha =1$): query the angle database, apply one controlled $R_y$ per angle bit onto the flag wire, unquery. The same ladder realizes the coefficient angle databases of the input-model matrix (Table~\ref {tab:input-models}); the database box is again a slot, bindable to a gate truth table or a QRAM resource.}}{19}{figure.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:qram-circuit}{{7}{19}{Two QRAM idioms, drawn by the quantikz export. Left: a bare \texttt {Load}---one instruction on the address and data registers, $|a\rangle |d\rangle \mapsto |a\rangle |d \oplus M[a]\rangle $, with the table itself a runtime resource. Right: the diagonal block-encoding of an angle table ($D_{jj}=\alpha \cos (s\,d_j/2)$, here $s=\pi /4$, $\alpha =1$): query the angle database, apply one controlled $R_y$ per angle bit onto the flag wire, unquery. The same ladder realizes the coefficient angle databases of the input-model matrix (\tabref {tab:input-models}); the database box is again a slot, bindable to a gate truth table or a QRAM resource}{figure.caption.31}{}}
